{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788393773,"nonce":"ueRAnhCH+/Wt4R3G8EefluvfZLw=","title":"pawn-fixture with NO args silently wounds a pawn: RefuseStray guards strays, nothing guards the empty case","message":"## What\n\n`pawn-fixture` called with no arguments mutates game state. It wounds a\ncolonist, stacks four `Debug bad thought` memories (-40 mood) and destroys\nthree apparel items' durability — with no confirmation and no dry-run.\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/PawnFixtureVerbs.cs:43-51`:\n\n    var steps = ctx.Args.StrList(\"steps\");\n    // Same shape as `journal-selftest`, same guard (git-bug 7382bdd\n    // comment #7): a defaulted, non-empty step list whose every step\n    // mutates — `wound` damages a pawn, `sadden` stacks thoughts,\n    // `tatter` destroys apparel durability. Refused before the first\n    // step rather than after the last.\n    ctx.Args.RefuseStray(\"pawn-fixture\", FixtureArgs,\n        \"Refused BEFORE any step ran, so nothing was mutated.\");\n    if (steps.Count == 0) steps = new List\u003cstring\u003e { \"wound\", \"sadden\", \"tatter\" };\n\n`RefuseStray` rejects UNKNOWN args. It does not reject the EMPTY arg set, and\nthe very next line turns \"the caller named no steps\" into \"run all three\nmutating steps\". The comment above the guard shows the hazard was understood;\nthe guard just does not cover this path.\n\nThe target is chosen by the verb too (`pawn_id` is optional), so a bare call\npicks its own victim.\n\n## How it bit\n\nRun `openrun-20260902`, day 9, tick 1605403. The agent was discovering verb\nsignatures by calling verbs bare and reading the `bad-args` refusal — safe and\nidiomatic for every verb with a required arg, which is nearly all of them\n(`assign`, `move-to`, `find-rect`, `quest`, `work-priorities` all refuse\ncleanly and mutate nothing). `pawn-fixture` is the one verb where that probe\nis destructive. Walton took 5 injuries at bleed 1.12, dropped to 73% health,\nand his three synthread garments went 89% -\u003e 15% on a map whose Cloth stock is\n0 and whose cotton crop had just died — i.e. unreplaceable. The -40 mood then\ndrove him to a Critical major-break risk while mining 78 cells from base.\n\nRepairing it took four more god-hand calls (`dev:heal`, `dev:spawn-thing` x3,\n`dev:set-need`). One accidental call cost six cheats in a play run.\n\n## Fix\n\nRefuse the empty-args call. A fixture that mutates three ways should require\nthe caller to say so:\n\n- `steps` becomes REQUIRED. No default. `pawn-fixture` with no args returns\n  `ok:false`, `error.class:\"refused\"`, and a detail listing the valid steps\n  and their effects.\n- Keep `RefuseStray` as-is; it is correct for what it covers.\n- Same audit for `world-fixture` and any other verb that defaults a non-empty\n  mutating work list — `journal-selftest` is named in the comment as the same\n  shape and should be checked.\n\nThe general rule this is an instance of: **a verb whose no-arg call mutates is\nindistinguishable from a read until after it has run.** Signature discovery by\nbare call is the natural way to use this surface; every verb that punishes it\nis a trap.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `pawn-fixture` with no args returns `ok:false`, `error.class:\"refused\"`, and\n  `error.detail` naming `steps` as required and listing `wound`/`sadden`/\n  `tatter` with a one-line effect each.\n- After that refusal every pawn's `hediffs`, `mood.thoughts` and worn-apparel\n  `hp_pct` are unchanged — asserted, not assumed.\n- `pawn-fixture {steps:[\"wound\"]}` still works and runs ONLY `wound`.\n- The same assertion exists for `world-fixture` and `journal-selftest`: a\n  no-arg call either mutates nothing or refuses.\n- An rwtest case covers the bare call for all three verbs (5.1 `7c83d6c`).","files":null}]}